      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "92cb9643-23c6-52fe-adf5-2ddc47e7c914",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$\n- **II.** $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y),3{x}^{3}⟩$\n- **III.** $R(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x+y}$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to values in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
