    "question": {
      ":question/id": "621c2cd5-6296-59e4-9c6b-1625a133c15b",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L(x,y,z)=2\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(y+x)\\,k$\n- **II.** $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-\\sqrt{y}\\,j$\n- **III.** $A(x,y,z)=x(y+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $L(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
