    "question": {
      ":question/id": "13598b62-b0ec-51cf-a60b-170559fe0615",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(x,y,z)=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$\n- **II.** $K(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$\n- **III.** $L(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $K(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $L(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
