      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $V(x,y,z)=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$\n- **II.** $R(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{4-x},4{z}^{3}⟩$\n- **III.** $C(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},\\sqrt{x+y}⟩$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,4-x\\ge 0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
