  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172087,
      "id": "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb",
      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "208d5226-118c-5268-8fde-cfd6bedf934c",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $V(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,⟩$\n- **II.** $G(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$\n- **III.** $N(x,y,z)=⟨z-{x}^{2},6x+{z}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $V(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "81f8493d-cc6e-5a98-b878-5ee3217f52aa",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
